]]></content:encoded></item><item><title>About</title><link>http://localhost:1313/blog/about/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>http://localhost:1313/blog/about/</guid><description>Otmane Echaibi, an amateur mathematician from Morocco who rebuilds mathematical ideas from the question that started them.</description><content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>I&rsquo;m <strong>Otmane Echaibi</strong>, I&rsquo;m 30, I live in Morocco, and I do mathematics for fun. Nobody pays me for it and nobody grades it, which turns out to be the best way to do it.</p>
<p>I went through the Moroccan system: the classes préparatoires (CPGE), then university lectures. A theorem arrived as a definition, then a lemma, then a proof, and the question that made anyone care never showed up. I learned to pass the exams. Somewhere along the way I nearly forgot that people once found this stuff thrilling, and I think that forgetting happens to a lot of talented people. This blog is my attempt to undo it, for myself first.</p>
<h2 id="how-a-post-works">How a post works</h2>
<p>Each post takes one idea from mathematics, usually from an article that is correct and almost unreadable, and rebuilds it the way it was found. There is a question, a few wrong guesses, a dead end, and then the idea shows up as the thing that repairs the damage. You will be asked to predict things. You will often be wrong. That is on purpose, because being wrong in a specific way is how an idea becomes yours.</p>
<h2 id="what-you-need">What you need</h2>
<p>A high school education, and it does not matter if you were bored by it. Every symbol is explained in words before it is used, and every technical term appears only after its meaning has done some work. When I give intuition instead of a proof, I say so. When I leave something out, I say that too.</p>
<h2 id="what-i-believe">What I believe</h2>
<p>Mathematics is something you do, closer to music or chess than to a list of procedures. Talent in it is common and easily discouraged. I want this to be the most accessible math blog I can make, and I would rather lose a reader to a hard idea explained slowly than to an easy one explained badly.</p>
<p>I am an amateur, and I will sometimes be wrong. If you catch me, tell me.</p>
<h2 id="say-hello">Say hello</h2>
<p>Topic suggestions, corrections, questions and friendly arguments are all welcome on the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded></item><item><title>Contact</title><link>http://localhost:1313/blog/contact/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>http://localhost:1313/blog/contact/</guid><description>Contact Otmane Echaibi: topic suggestions, corrections, questions, or a good argument about mathematics.</description><content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Got stuck on a proof that every textbook skips? Found a mistake in one of my posts? Know a piece of mathematics that deserves a better explanation than the one it has? Write to me.</p>
<h2 id="reach-me">Reach me</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Email:</strong> <code>grumble-chief-eats@duck.com</code></li>
<li><strong>GitHub:</strong> <code>https://github.com/otmnsh/</code></li>
</ul>
<h2 id="suggest-a-topic">Suggest a topic</h2>
<p>Send me a Wikipedia link or just a name, plus a line about where you got lost or what bothered you about it. Confusion is the most useful raw material I get. I will credit you if you like.</p>
<h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
<p>Mathematics tolerates no small errors, and I am an amateur. If something in a post is wrong, unclear, or claims more than it proves, tell me which post, which line, and what you think is right. I will fix it and credit you if you like.</p>
<h2 id="response-time">Response time</h2>
<p>I&rsquo;m a one-person blog with a day job, so replies can take a few days. I read everything and answer every note.</p>
]]></content:encoded></item><item><title>Privacy Policy</title><link>http://localhost:1313/blog/privacy/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>http://localhost:1313/blog/privacy/</guid><description>What this blog does and does not collect.</description><content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Last updated:</strong> September 2026</p>
<h2 id="the-short-version">The short version</h2>
<p>This blog does not use analytics, advertising, tracking cookies, or comment systems, and it does not ask you to create an account. There is very little to say here, on purpose.</p>
<h2 id="what-happens-when-you-visit">What happens when you visit</h2>
<p>This site is a set of static pages served by GitLab Pages. When you load a page, your browser requests files directly from GitLab&rsquo;s infrastructure, which, like any web host, logs standard technical data (such as IP address, browser type, and requested URL) for operation and security. I do not receive, store, or use that data myself. See <a href="https://about.gitlab.com/privacy/">GitLab&rsquo;s privacy statement</a> for how they handle it.</p>
<ul>
<li>No first-party cookies are set by this site.</li>
<li>No third-party trackers, ad networks, or social-media pixels are embedded.</li>
<li>The search page runs entirely in your browser against an index built when the site is generated. Your search terms never leave your device.</li>
<li>The light/dark theme choice is remembered using your browser&rsquo;s local storage, on your device only.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="if-this-changes">If this changes</h2>
<p>If I ever add privacy-respecting, cookie-less analytics, I will update this page first.</p>
<h2 id="links-to-other-sites">Links to other sites</h2>
<p>Posts may link to outside sources. Once you click through, that site&rsquo;s own privacy policy applies.</p>
<h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
<p>Questions about this policy? See the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded></item></channel></rss>